ALERT SKIPPED at the publish site, not merely down-weighted: index.mjs:176 is
`shouldAlert(card) ? putPerformanceEvent(card, cardKey) : Promise.resolve()`, so the
events-table row is never written, while publishMetrics(card) still runs -- the
CloudWatch datapoints and the card itself survive, only the alerting input drops.
computeBands additionally returns status 'ok', noOp: true and anomalies: [] (:567-580),
so a reader cannot mistake "not compared" for "compared, fine".

NON-VACUITY throughout: 'deploy-blocked' is terminal and NOT a no-op on all three
surfaces (a run that did the work then failed to ship stays a comparable), a $0 card is
rejected for the pre-existing reason, and a genuinely tiny 'complete' run still alerts
(index.test.mjs:415-421) -- so the exclusion keys on the outcome, not on smallness.

ONE DOCUMENTED CONSEQUENCE, stated in the source rather than found by me
(index.mjs:533-535): "anything counting runs by workflow.performance rows under-counts
no-op sweeps, by design." Anyone building run-volume dashboards off that event type
will be short by the number of no-op sweeps. It is written down; it is not tested.

